WEBVTT

NOTE Source: final 2026-09-27 English narration; provider word times mapped through applied cue tempo.

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.940
ACCRUE: Continual Off-Policy
Reinforcement Learning for

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.720
Robot Foundation Models.

3
00:00:06.161 --> 00:00:10.412
Single-task RL learns each
task from scratch, repeating

4
00:00:10.412 --> 00:00:12.192
costly robot interaction.

5
00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:15.852
ACCRUE carries that
experience forward to learn

6
00:00:15.852 --> 00:00:17.332
new tasks faster.

7
00:00:18.077 --> 00:00:21.725
Accumulated demonstrations
update the foundation model.

8
00:00:21.725 --> 00:00:25.616
During RL, a compact
actor–critic refines the frozen

9
00:00:25.616 --> 00:00:28.013
model’s references into proposed actions.

10
00:00:28.096 --> 00:00:30.059
Both networks carry forward.

11
00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:33.150
With limited prior replay,
adapting the critic can

12
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.406
make earlier-task values unreliable.

13
00:00:35.518 --> 00:00:39.572
In ACCRUE, it guides only
current-task actor updates.

14
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:43.080
Before RL, we cache
previous actor outputs;

15
00:00:43.297 --> 00:00:45.712
during training, we distill those targets.

16
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.564
Simulation benchmarks
pair GR00T with FastSAC:

17
00:00:49.941 --> 00:00:52.645
FORGE covers peg, gear, and nut assembly;

18
00:00:52.648 --> 00:00:54.992
AutoMate covers plug-and-socket assembly.

19
00:00:55.161 --> 00:00:57.848
We learn peg insertion, gear meshing,

20
00:00:57.956 --> 00:00:59.048
then nut threading.

21
00:01:00.082 --> 00:01:02.902
The final policy revisits earlier tasks.

22
00:01:03.159 --> 00:01:05.777
Forward transfer measures
learning-curve area.

23
00:01:05.852 --> 00:01:08.913
Demonstration-only is
supervised fine-tuning.

24
00:01:09.103 --> 00:01:11.748
Single-task RL starts fresh.

25
00:01:11.962 --> 00:01:15.108
ACCRUE carries experience
forward and achieves

26
00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:16.602
higher forward transfer.

27
00:01:17.156 --> 00:01:21.552
We compare sequential learning,
weight regularization, actor blending,

28
00:01:21.684 --> 00:01:22.875
and experience replay.

29
00:01:23.162 --> 00:01:26.042
Only ACCRUE succeeds at peg insertion in

30
00:01:26.042 --> 00:01:27.310
this matched example.

31
00:01:28.161 --> 00:01:31.676
Across these examples,
only ACCRUE succeeds at

32
00:01:31.676 --> 00:01:33.072
both earlier tasks.

33
00:01:34.078 --> 00:01:38.640
Negative backward transfer measures
average earlier-task success loss.

34
00:01:38.764 --> 00:01:40.388
ACCRUE forgets less.

35
00:01:40.477 --> 00:01:43.124
It also achieves higher
final success across the

36
00:01:43.124 --> 00:01:44.502
full task sequence.

37
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.978
Real robots pair
pi-zero-point-five with FastTD3.

38
00:01:49.162 --> 00:01:53.026
Here, we compare single-task
RL with ACCRUE on

39
00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:55.138
five successive AutoMate assemblies.

40
00:01:55.507 --> 00:01:57.992
ACCRUE supports different
foundation models and

41
00:01:57.992 --> 00:02:00.408
off-policy actor–critic algorithms.

42
00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:03.629
Both methods are stopped at
ninety-five percent success

43
00:02:03.629 --> 00:02:05.438
over the latest twenty episodes.

44
00:02:05.545 --> 00:02:08.551
Blue curves show single-task RL;

45
00:02:08.551 --> 00:02:11.745
the horizontal axis measures
robot interaction time.

46
00:02:12.062 --> 00:02:16.109
For subsequent tasks, ACCRUE
needs thirty-one to sixty-eight

47
00:02:16.109 --> 00:02:18.762
point five percent less robot interaction

48
00:02:18.762 --> 00:02:20.622
than single-task RL.

49
00:02:22.394 --> 00:02:26.688
After all five tasks, separate
evaluations show ninety-five

50
00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:29.721
to one hundred percent
success on earlier assemblies.

51
00:02:31.162 --> 00:02:34.302
The NIST tasks test a separate sequence:

52
00:02:34.655 --> 00:02:38.142
rectangular peg insertion,
cylindrical peg insertion,

53
00:02:38.298 --> 00:02:39.502
then gear meshing.

54
00:02:39.578 --> 00:02:42.622
Blue curves again show
single-task learning.

55
00:02:43.729 --> 00:02:47.369
ACCRUE needs roughly forty
percent less robot interaction.

56
00:02:48.392 --> 00:02:52.279
After gear meshing, both
earlier insertion skills remain.

57
00:02:55.162 --> 00:02:57.035
See our paper for ablations of

58
00:02:57.035 --> 00:02:58.521
inheritance and replay.
